# IMPERIA CW — Cold War: West Germany integration from quaZa e45483ff.
"""Flavor texts: Deutschlandpolitik."""
D = {
"dp_hub": "Twenty years of non-recognition have made the Wall higher, not lower. The Hallstein Doctrine is crumbling as one country after another recognises the GDR, and families in Berlin have not seen each other since August 1961. Bonn must decide what it wants from the other German state - recognition, rapprochement or pressure - and how hard it is prepared to push.",
"wandel_durch_annaeherung": "Tutzing, July 1963: Egon Bahr, Brandt's press spokesman in West Berlin, offers a formula that will shape two decades - change through rapprochement. Accept the realities in order to change them; small steps for people instead of big words about unity. The Union calls it capitulation. The people waiting at the crossing points call it hope.",
"bahr_paper": "Ten points, written in Moscow in the spring of 1970 by a state secretary who negotiated with Andrei Gromyko as if he were foreign minister: the renunciation of force, the inviolability of the borders, the sequence of treaties to come. The paper was meant to remain secret. Leaked to the Bild-Zeitung, it becomes the programme - and the opposition's favourite target.",
"erfurt_kassel": "March 1970: for the first time, the heads of government of the two German states meet. In Erfurt the crowds break through the police lines and chant 'Willy Brandt ans Fenster!' - Willy Brandt to the window. He appears and raises his hands to calm them. The SED is horrified. In Kassel, two months later, the return visit ends in protests and a torn-down flag.",
"moscow_treaty": "August 1970: in the Kremlin, Brandt and Kosygin sign a treaty on the renunciation of force and the recognition of the borders of Europe as they are - including the Oder-Neisse line and the border between the two German states. Attached is a letter from the Federal Government: the treaty does not contradict the goal of German unity. Moscow accepts the letter without comment.",
"warsaw_treaty": "December 1970: the Treaty of Warsaw recognises the Oder-Neisse line as Poland's western border. Before signing it, Willy Brandt lays a wreath at the memorial to the heroes of the Warsaw Ghetto - and falls to his knees. The photograph travels around the world. At home, a majority of West Germans think the kneeling was exaggerated. History will disagree.",
"ratification": "The coalition's majority has melted away through defections; a constructive vote of no confidence against Brandt fails by two votes - votes that, as will later emerge, the Stasi may have bought. Now the coalition must put the Eastern Treaties to the Bundestag. Without them, détente collapses. With them, the opposition will claim that Germany has been sold.",
"basic_treaty": "December 1972: the Basic Treaty between the two German states. Good-neighbourly relations, permanent representations instead of embassies, cooperation in trade, transport and culture - and a formula on which both sides can agree because each reads it differently: two states in one German nation. Bavaria takes it to the Constitutional Court. The court lets it stand, with conditions.",
"prague_treaty": "The Munich Agreement of 1938, by which Hitler took the Sudetenland, is declared void - though when exactly it became void is a question the lawyers argue about for months. Diplomatic relations with Czechoslovakia follow, and with Hungary and Bulgaria. The last chapter of the Eastern Treaties is written. The Sudeten German associations call it a betrayal.",
"helsinki": "August 1975, Helsinki: thirty-five heads of government sign the Final Act of the Conference on Security and Cooperation in Europe. Moscow gets what it wanted - the borders of post-war Europe accepted by the West. The West gets something that seems harmless: human rights and freedom of movement written down in Basket Three. Dissidents in the East will hold their rulers to every word.",
"prisoner_buyouts": "Since 1963 the Federal Republic has quietly paid for the release of political prisoners from the GDR: in cash, in goods, in deliveries of oil and coffee through the Protestant churches' accounts. By 1989 tens of thousands of people will have been bought free. It is a trade in human beings, say the critics. For the prisoners it is a way out of Bautzen.",
"swing_credit": "Inner-German trade runs on an interest-free overdraft, the 'Swing', which allows the GDR to buy more from the West than it sells. Raise the ceiling and East Berlin becomes a little more dependent on Bonn's goodwill - and a little more willing to grant concessions on travel and family visits. Lower it, and the planners in East Berlin feel it within a quarter.",
"church_contacts": "The Protestant churches in East and West never fully separated. Their synods meet, their parishes are twinned, their money flows across the border. Under the roof of the church in the GDR, peace groups, environmental circles and human rights activists find the only space the SED does not control. Supporting the churches means supporting the opposition - without saying so.",
"west_media_strategy": "ARD and ZDF can be watched across the entire GDR except for the 'valley of the clueless' around Dresden. Every evening at eight, millions of East Germans watch the Tagesschau and know more about the Federal Republic than about their own country. The West comes in through the aerial. Bonn can decide whether to make programmes that are meant for the East.",
"coalition_of_reason": "Two German leaders who agree on one thing: that war must never again start from German soil. Missiles are being deployed on both sides of the border, the superpowers have stopped talking - and the chancellor and the General Secretary keep talking anyway. A 'coalition of reason' across the Wall: the SED needs Bonn's money, Bonn needs the SED's restraint.",
"community_of_responsibility": "The two German states share a responsibility for peace in Europe - a phrase that East Berlin and Bonn both use, for very different reasons. For the SED it is a step towards recognition as a sovereign state; for Bonn, a reminder that the Germans remain one nation. In a decade of new missiles, it is one of the few things they can agree on.",
"embassy_refugees": "Summer 1989: East Germans climb the fences of the West German embassies in Prague, Budapest and Warsaw and camp in the gardens by the thousand. Every one of them, under the Basic Law, is a citizen of the Federal Republic. On 30 September Genscher appears on the balcony in Prague: 'We have come to tell you that today your departure...' The rest is drowned in cheers.",
"rechtsposition": "The preamble of the Basic Law, one German citizenship for all Germans, the German Reich in its borders of 1937 as the legal starting point: Bonn may talk to East Berlin, trade with it and sign treaties with it, but it gives nothing away in law. The legal position is a fortress of paper. One day, Bonn hopes, it will matter.",
"letter_on_unity": "Handed over with every treaty, from Moscow to the Basic Treaty: the letter in which the Federal Government states that its goal remains a state of peace in Europe in which the German people regain their unity in free self-determination. The other side never answers it, never rejects it - and never forgets it was received.",
"billion_credit": "July 1983: Franz Josef Strauß, of all people, arranges a state-guaranteed loan of a billion marks for a GDR that is on the brink of insolvency. Honecker's regime is saved from bankruptcy. In return the automatic firing devices on the border are dismantled and the controls on transit routes are eased. The CSU's base is appalled. The border becomes a little less deadly.",
"salzgitter": "Since 1961 a central registry at the public prosecutor's office in Salzgitter, in Lower Saxony, has recorded every shot fired at the border, every death at the Wall and every political sentence in the GDR. The files are meant for the day when the guilty can be brought to trial. East Berlin demands its closure as the price for better relations. Bonn has to decide what the files are worth.",
"magnet_theory": "Make the West so attractive that the East is drawn to it like iron to a magnet: no deals with the SED, no credits, no recognition - only prosperity, freedom and patient pressure. It is the doctrine of the Adenauer years. Its supporters argue that it was abandoned too soon; its critics that it built the Wall.",
"csce_human_rights": "Basket Three of the Helsinki Final Act is quoted back at the SED at every follow-up conference: freedom of movement, family reunification, the free flow of information. In Belgrade, Madrid and Vienna the Western delegations read out the names of East Germans who have been refused permission to leave. The SED signed the Final Act. Now it must live with it.",
"confrontation_course": "No recognition, no credits, no illusions: the GDR is a Soviet satrapy that will collapse if it is pressed hard enough - and Bonn will press. The policy of strength promises that the Germans in the East will not be abandoned to their rulers. It also promises a colder, more dangerous border and an East Berlin with nothing left to lose.",
"renegotiate_treaties": "Chancellor Barzel wants the treaties - Bonn cannot afford to look like the enemy of détente - but on better terms: explicit reservations on German unity, on the rights of the expellees, on the status of Berlin. Moscow refuses to reopen signed agreements. The question is whether Bonn has enough to offer to make it change its mind.",
"sole_mandate_renewed": "Bonn alone speaks for Germany. Any state that recognises the GDR will lose its West German aid and its West German ambassador: the Hallstein Doctrine, revived. In 1955 it worked; in the 1970s the Third World has other options, and the GDR has friends with money of their own. The question is how many friends Bonn is prepared to lose.",
"economic_war": "Close the back door through which the GDR's goods enter the Common Market duty-free, freeze the Swing, end the credits, stop the purchase of prisoners: let the planners in East Berlin discover what the Deutschmark was really worth to them. Economic warfare could bring the regime to its knees. It would also hit the people it is meant to help first.",
"escape_as_policy": "Tunnels under the Wall, forged passports, cars with hidden compartments, escape helpers who charge ten thousand marks a head: what was tolerated becomes encouraged, and the Federal Republic makes the escape of its fellow Germans a reason of state. East Berlin calls it 'trafficking in human beings' and demands that Bonn stop it. Bonn does not.",
"radio_war": "New transmitters on the western side of the Brocken, RIAS Berlin on full power, programmes made especially for the East - news, rock music, the voices of those who fled. The airwaves war is cheap, effective and impossible to stop with a wall. The GDR builds jamming transmitters. The young people of Leipzig and Rostock tune in anyway.",
"liberation_doctrine": "If the GDR breaks apart - as it nearly did in June 1953 - Bonn will not stand by as it did then, or as the West did in Budapest in 1956. A doctrine for the day after: recognition of a free government in the East, help, and if necessary more than help. A doctrine for the day before as well. Moscow reads it as a declaration of intent.",
"crackdown_response": "The SED has fired on its own people. The images from the streets of Leipzig and East Berlin are on every screen in the world. Bonn must decide: sanction the regime, cut every credit and recall the Permanent Representative - or keep the channels open for the sake of the prisoners and the families, as every government before has done.",
"second_cold_war": "The coup in Moscow has succeeded, or the Soviets have said no to unity, or the reform in the East has been crushed. Whatever the cause, divided Germany prepares for a long winter: the Wall stays, the missiles stay, the dream of the autumn is over. History has offered a window, and the window has closed.",
"ten_point_plan": "28 November 1989: without telling his allies or his coalition partner, Helmut Kohl presents a ten-point plan to the Bundestag - aid for the GDR, confederative structures between the two states, and finally a federation. Washington is surprised, Paris and London are furious, Moscow warns. The chancellor has seized the initiative and will not let it go.",
"support_round_table": "At the Round Table in East Berlin the SED sits opposite the new opposition: New Forum, Democracy Now, the Social Democrats of the East, pastors and environmental activists. They have courage but no money, no offices and no printing presses. Bonn sends them all three - quietly, through the party foundations, so that the SED cannot call them Western puppets.",
"treaty_community": "Hans Modrow, the reform communist, offers a contractual community between two sovereign states, then a confederation, and unity at the end - in years, not months. It is a serious offer from a serious man. The people in the streets have stopped chanting 'We are the people' and now chant 'We are one people'. They do not want to wait years.",
"art146_debate": "Article 146: the Basic Law shall cease to apply on the day on which a constitution freely adopted by the German people comes into force. The fathers of the Basic Law meant it for the day of unity. Draft a new constitution together, say the civil rights movements of the East and the left of the West. Join the Basic Law under Article 23, say Kohl and the Union - quickly, before the window closes.",
"neutral_offer": "Stalin offered it in March 1952: a united, neutral Germany with its own army, in return for leaving the Western alliance. Adenauer refused without even testing the offer. If Moscow offers it again, should Bonn take it - unity at the price of NATO? The peace movement says yes. Washington, Paris and London say no in a single voice.",
"oder_neisse_final": "No unity without a final border. Poland demands a treaty, not a promise; the expellees' associations demand that the question remain open; Kohl hesitates for months, worrying about the votes of the expellees. In the end the Bundestag and the Volkskammer declare the Oder-Neisse line final, and Warsaw finally believes that Germany means it.",
"unity_treaty": "Nine hundred pages that merge two legal systems, two pension systems, two education systems and two armies. Wolfgang Schäuble and Günther Krause negotiate it in a few weeks: property questions, abortion law, the fate of the Stasi files, the capital. Never has a treaty of this size been written so quickly - and never will one have so many consequences.",
"soviet_withdrawal": "Three hundred and forty thousand Soviet soldiers and their families are stationed in the GDR. They must go home - to a country that has no flats, no barracks and no jobs for them. Bonn pays twelve billion marks to house them and a few billion more for their transport. The last Soviet soldiers leave Berlin on 31 August 1994. The Germans are free.",
"stasi_files": "Six million files, a hundred and eighty kilometres of shelves, the record of a whole society spied upon by itself. Every citizen may now read what the Ministry for State Security wrote about them - and learn which friend, which colleague, which husband informed on them. The Gauck authority opens in January 1992. The country begins to read.",
"eastern_relations": "After Moscow, Warsaw and Prague, the rest of the Warsaw Pact: diplomatic relations with Budapest and Sofia in December 1973 end the last traces of the Hallstein Doctrine. West German ambassadors now sit in every capital of the East. Trade follows, exchange follows - and with them, quietly, the influence of the richest country in Europe.",
"romanian_germans": "Ceaușescu's Romania sells its ethnic Germans: so many thousand marks for every Transylvanian Saxon and Banat Swabian allowed to leave, paid through secret accounts and negotiated by intelligence officers in hotel rooms. Bonn pays, quietly, and asks no questions about where the money goes. Over the years, two hundred thousand people come to the Federal Republic this way.",
"polish_emigration": "Helsinki, August 1975: at the margins of the CSCE summit Schmidt and Gierek agree on a package - billions of marks in credits and a lump sum for Polish pension claims, and in return exit permits for 125,000 ethnic Germans over four years. The opposition calls it buying people; the Polish government calls it compensation. The families call it home.",
"yugoslav_partnership": "Half a million Yugoslav guest workers live in the Federal Republic, and Tito's non-aligned Yugoslavia is the most open country of the socialist world. Belgrade wants credits instead of reparations for the occupation; Bonn wants Belgrade to stop sheltering terrorists and to hand over those it arrests. A partnership of interest, with Tito's death looming over it.",
"solidarity_support": "December 1981: General Jaruzelski declares martial law in Poland, Solidarność is banned and its leaders interned. Millions of West Germans send food parcels to Poland; the government sends money to the Catholic Church. The question in Bonn is whether it should also speak out for Solidarność - and what that would cost détente.",
"quiet_diplomacy_poland": "On 13 December 1981, the day martial law is declared in Poland, Helmut Schmidt is at Honecker's side at the Werbellinsee - and he stays for the talks. Détente, he argues, must not become a casualty of Warsaw; shouting would not free a single internee. The Americans are appalled. The chancellor believes that quiet diplomacy saves more people than loud protest.",
"soviet_germans": "Two million ethnic Germans live in Kazakhstan, Siberia and Kyrgyzstan, the descendants of those Stalin deported from the Volga in 1941. Under Gorbachev the gates begin to open - if Bonn asks, and if Bonn is prepared to receive them. Hundreds of thousands come every year at the peak: Germans by law, strangers in the country of their ancestors.",
"bonn_declaration_1989": "June 1989: Mikhail Gorbachev in Bonn, crowds on the market square chanting 'Gorbi! Gorbi!' as if he were a pop star. The joint declaration recognises the right of every people to choose its own political system. The Soviet leader means Poland and Hungary. In East Berlin, the Politburo reads the same sentence with growing alarm.",
"hungarian_border": "May 1989: Hungary begins cutting the barbed wire on its border with Austria. Thousands of East Germans on holiday at Lake Balaton camp in Budapest, waiting for the door to open. Bonn can help Budapest decide - with a credit of five hundred million marks, and with the assurance that Hungary will not be left alone when East Berlin and Moscow protest.",
"european_peace_order": "Not reunification against the East, but a peace order in Europe in which the German question can be solved by consent: the old dream of Brandt and Bahr. It needs years of trust between the blocs, treaties that work in practice, and four victorious powers that are prepared to let the Germans decide their own fate. It is the long road. It may be the only one that leads anywhere.",
"social_union": "Pensions, health insurance and unemployment benefit for all Germans on the same terms: the offer of a social union makes a mixed economic order acceptable to the workers of the East, who fear that unity will cost them their jobs and their security. It is also an enormous bill for the West's taxpayers - and the bill will arrive whether or not unity does.",
"berlin_pledge": "Bonn was always meant to be provisional - a 'federal village' on the Rhine until the day of unity. A pledge that a united Germany will govern from Berlin wins hearts in the East, where the old capital stands for the whole nation. It loses some on the Rhine, where the civil servants have built their houses and the Rhineland has grown fond of its republic.",
"withdrawal_fund": "Barracks, flats, retraining and pensions for the soldiers of the Group of Soviet Forces in Germany, paid for by Bonn - in advance. Moscow cannot afford to bring its army home; the Soviet economy is collapsing. A fund for the withdrawal is the price of the Kremlin's signature under any treaty on unity. It is the cheapest liberation in history.",
"four_power_conference": "Bonn invites the foreign ministers of the Four Powers - the United States, the Soviet Union, Britain and France - to sit with both German states at one table. Two plus four, not four plus two: the Germans negotiate their own future, but nothing is signed until the victors of 1945 have given up their rights. It is the formula that will end the post-war era.",
"both_peoples_decide": "A referendum on the same day in both German states: if the Germans themselves vote for the treaty of unity, no parliament, no opposition and no foreign government can stand in its way. It is the most democratic way to unite a nation. It is also a gamble - for if either people says no, the question may be closed for a generation.",
}
